  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 1119 AM MST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nIt's rather quiet across the forecast area today. Current\nsatellite shows a few high clouds across the northern mountains\nand along the Cheyenne Ridge, with mostly sunny skies elsewhere.\nTemperatures are running a couple degrees cooler than this time\nyesterday, but should still reach the mid 60s across most of the\nDenver metro and plains.\n\nThe ridge off to our west (currently centered near the UT/NV\nborder) should slide eastward for Thursday and Friday. The dry\nairmass and very weak flow aloft should allow for our quiet period\nto continue through the rest of the week, with highs again several\ndegrees above normal.\n\nCooler weather is expected this weekend, as the first of a couple\nshortwaves moves through the region. This initial shortwave \nbreaks off from a broad longwave trough on Saturday, eventually \nforming a weak closed low near the OK panhandle or northern Texas \nby Sunday morning. This system is generally moisture starved and \nlacks any significant QG ascent as it passes through the region. \nWeak mid/upper flow aloft will also limit the \nstrength/organization of any orographic snow across the mountains,\nthough we still generally expect some light accumulations \n(especially along/south of I-70). Though a few model cycles hinted\nat a stronger/better organized upper low Saturday night... which \nled to some decent QPF across portions of our plains... most \nguidance has backed off on that and it looks like it'll be \ndifficult to get any precipitation this far north. The NBM PoP \ngradient Saturday night is probably a bit too broad and displaced \ntoo far northeast, but time will tell as this system starts \ngetting inside the regional/mesoscale model windows.\n\nGenerally quiet weather is expected again for the first couple\ndays of next week, as weak ridging builds back across the region.\nHowever, this looks to be short-lived as we enter what appears to\nbe a cooler, more active weather pattern from mid-week towards\nnext weekend. The first prominent shortwave is currently expected\nto track towards our forecast area by Wednesday. Model consistency\nin this period is poor, but it will be something to keep an eye on\nover the next several days.",
